>
  <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> most school and college students learn well with <strong>2–4 hours of focused study a day</strong> outside class, and <strong>5–7 hours on free days</strong> in the final weeks before big exams. Beyond that, extra hours add less and less, especially if they come out of your sleep.</p>
  <p>The right number depends on your course, your exams and how much of that time is truly focused. This guide gives you ranges, the research behind them, and a 4-step way to work out your own number.</p>

  <h2 id="short">The short answer (by student type)</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Student</th><th scope="col">Normal school/college day</th><th scope="col">Weekend or holiday</th><th scope="col">Final 4–6 weeks before exams</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Class 6–8</td><td>1–1.5 h</td><td>2 h</td><td>2–3 h</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Class 9–10</td><td>2–3 h</td><td>3–4 h</td><td>4–6 h on holidays</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Class 11–12</td><td>2.5–4 h</td><td>4–5 h</td><td>5–7 h on holidays</td></tr>
      <tr><td>College / university</td><td>2–4 h</td><td>3–5 h</td><td>5–7 h in study leave</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Full-time entrance-exam preparation</td><td colspan="3">6–8 h, split into blocks with breaks and one lighter day a week</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>These are starting points we use when building plans, not rules. If you are on track and sleeping well, you do not need more. If a subject keeps slipping, add a block for that subject, not hours across the board.</p>
  <p>For context: a large US study of time-use surveys found full-time college students spent about <strong>40 hours a week</strong> on classes plus study in 1961, falling to about <strong>27 hours</strong> by 2003 (Babcock &amp; Marks, 2011). Hours alone do not decide grades, but very low study time is hard to make up later.</p>

  <h2 id="quality">Why focused hours beat total hours</h2>
  <p>Three findings explain why “how many hours” is the wrong question on its own.</p>
  <h3>1. Real concentration has a daily limit</h3>
  <p>In a classic study of expert violinists, the best performers kept their deliberate practice, the hard, focused kind, to <strong>no more than about four hours a day</strong>, spread across sessions with rest in between (Ericsson et al., 1993). Studying a new topic or solving problems is similarly demanding. After a few hours, most people drift into rereading and highlighting, which a major review rated as low-utility techniques (Dunlosky et al., 2013).</p>
  <h3>2. Spreading the hours out works better</h3>
  <p>The same total time spread across several days is remembered far better than one long session. In a study of over 1,300 people, the best gap between study sessions was roughly <strong>20–40% of the time until the test</strong> for a test one week away, falling to 5–10% for a test a year away (Cepeda et al., 2008). Two hours a day for five days beats ten hours on Sunday.</p>
  <h3>3. What you do in the hour matters most</h3>
  <p>An hour of self-testing (past papers, flashcards, explaining from memory) teaches more than an hour of rereading. Practice testing and spaced practice were the only two techniques rated “high utility” in Dunlosky and colleagues’ review. Read more in <a href="/guides/evidence-based-study-techniques/">what the research says about study techniques</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="work-out">Work out your own number in 4 steps</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Estimate the work.</strong> For each exam, guess the total hours you need: for example 30 hours for a big final, 10 for a unit test. Be honest about weak subjects.</li>
    <li><strong>Count real study days.</strong> Days until the exam, minus one rest day a week and days already full. The <a href="/tools/exam-countdown/">exam countdown</a> does this.</li>
    <li><strong>Divide.</strong> Total hours ÷ study days = hours per day. If the answer is above 6–7, start earlier, drop low-mark topics, or ask for help on the hardest one.</li>
    <li><strong>Turn hours into blocks.</strong> Split each day into 45–60 minute blocks with breaks, and give each block one subject and one task. The <a href="/tools/study-plan-generator/">study plan generator</a> builds the whole table.</li>
  </ol>
  <blockquote>Example: 3 exams needing 30, 25 and 15 hours = 70 hours. 28 days with Sundays off = 24 study days. 70 ÷ 24 ≈ 3 hours a day, or three one-hour blocks.</blockquote>

  <h2 id="sleep">Sleep: the hours you should not take</h2>
  <p>Studying late into the night feels productive but often backfires.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>In a study that followed high-school students day by day, students who <strong>gave up sleep to study more than usual</strong> had more trouble understanding lessons and more problems on tests the next day, whatever their usual study time (Gillen-O’Neel et al., 2013).</li>
    <li>In an MIT study of 88 college students wearing sleep trackers, sleep quality, duration and consistency together explained nearly <strong>25% of the differences in their grades</strong>. One good night before the test was not enough; sleep in the weeks before mattered (Okano et al., 2019).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Fix your sleep first, then add study hours on top.</p>

  <h2 id="week">What a good study day looks like</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Time</th><th scope="col">School/college day (3 h)</th><th scope="col">Holiday in exam season (6 h)</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Morning</td><td>—</td><td>9:00–10:30 hardest subject (new topics)<br />10:45–12:00 practice questions</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Afternoon</td><td>4:30–5:30 homework</td><td>2:00–3:15 second subject<br />3:30–4:30 past-paper section</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Evening</td><td>6:30–7:30 one subject, ending with a 10-min self-quiz<br />8:30–9:15 review / flashcards</td><td>6:30–7:30 review of the day’s topics<br />8:30 stop; light reading only</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Notice the pattern: hardest work first, short breaks between blocks, a self-test in every block, and a fixed stop time. Put the blocks in a planner so you do not spend the morning deciding what to do. ClassTrack shows them next to your classes and homework on your phone.</p>
</Guide>
